#{[17592186107048 1029 "We wish to prove the following formula for the variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nTo begin, recall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]\n$$\n\nExpanding out the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] =\\textrm E [ X^2 - 2\\textrm E[X] \\cdot X + \\textrm E[X]^2 ]\n$$\n\nThen, distributing the expectation over the sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ X^2 ] + \\textrm E[ - 2\\textrm E[X] \\cdot X ] + \\textrm E[ \\textrm E[X]^2 ]\n$$\n\nNow, we make the following simplifications:\n\n- Since $\\textrm E[aX] = a \\textrm E[X]$ for any constant $a$, we can write\n$\\textrm E[{\\color{blue}-2 \\textrm E[X]} \\cdot X] = {\\color{blue}- 2\\textrm E[X]} \\cdot \\textrm E[X] =-2\\textrm E[X]^2$.\n- Since $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is a constant, and $\\textrm E[a] = a$ for any constant $a$, we can write\n$\\textrm E[{\\color{blue}\\textrm E[X]^2}] = {\\color{blue}\\textrm E[X]^2}$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] + E[- 2E[X] \\cdot X] + E[E[X]^{2}] \\\\\n&= E[X^{2}] - 2E[X]^{2} + E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107049 1029 "Suppose we have a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f_X$ and support\n\n$$\nS_X = \\{-1,0,1\\}\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nNow suppose that we define a new random variable $Y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nY = (X+1)^3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ is quite a lot of work if we use only the methods we've seen up to now. Typically, we would need to find the probability mass function $f_Y$ of $Y$, and then compute $\\textrm{E}[Y]$.\n\nThankfully, there is a handy rule, called **the rule of the lazy statistician,** that allows us to compute $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ *without* having to find $f_Y$ first! This is useful because $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ might be the only quantity we're interested in.\n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician states that if $Y = r(X)$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x)\n$$\n\nApplying the rule of the lazy statistician with the function\n\n$$\nr(x) = (x+1)^3\n$$\n\nfor our PMF $f_X$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= \\sum _{x \\in  {S}_{X}}r(x)f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= \\sum _{x \\in  {S}_{X}}(x + 1)^{3}f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= (-1 + 1)^{3} \\cdot 0.25 + (0 + 1)^{3} \\cdot 0.5 + (1 + 1)^{3} \\cdot 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0^{3} \\cdot 0.25 + 1^{3} \\cdot 0.5 + 2^{3} \\cdot 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.25 + 1 \\cdot 0.5 + 8 \\cdot 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0 + 0.5 + 2 \\\\\n&= 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.5\n$$"] [17592186107050 1029 "Although\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[r(X)] \\ne r(\\textrm{E}[X])\n$$\n\nin general, equality is possible for some functions.\n\nFor example, we've already seen that if $a$ and $b$ are real constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[aX+b]=a \\, \\textrm{E}[X]+b\n$$\n\nLet's prove this using the rule of the lazy statistician for a discrete random variable $X$. In this case,\n\n$$\nr(X)=aX+b\n$$\n\n, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[aX + b] &= \\sum _{x \\in  S}(ax + b)f(x) \\\\\n&= \\sum _{x \\in  S}axf(x) + \\sum _{x \\in  S}bf(x) \\\\\n&= a\\sum _{x \\in  S}xf(x) + b\\sum _{x \\in  S}f(x) \\\\\n&= aE[X] + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA similar proof can be conducted for continuous random variables.\n\nSimilarly, for functions $r(x)$ and $s(x)$ and real coefficients $a$ and $b$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[a \\cdot r(X) + b \\cdot s(X)] = a \\, \\textrm{E}[r(X)] + b \\, \\textrm{E}[s(X)]\n$$"] [17592186107051 1029 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ and let $Y=r(X)$. Then, the rule of the lazy statistician for continuous random variables states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y]=\\textrm E[r(X)]=\\int^{\\infty}_{-\\infty} r(x)f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNotice the analogy with the discrete case."] [17592186107052 1029 "When working with polar coordinates, we primarily use the following domain for the polar angle $\\theta$:\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, it's sometimes a good idea to use the domain\n\n$$\n-\\pi \\leq \\theta \\lt \\pi\n$$\n\nChanging the domain for $\\theta$ turns out to be especially useful when a polar petal spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nLet's see an example."] [17592186107053 1029 "When $C$ is a closed curve, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" $C$. It is usually easy to determine which region is inside or outside an arbitrary closed curve using our natural intuition.\n\nOne important quantity that we often need when dealing with closed, oriented curves is the **outward-pointing unit normal vector,** usually denoted $\\mathbf n$.\n\nAs the name suggests:\n\n- $\\mathbf n$ is normal (i.e., perpendicular) to the curve $C$ at *every* point,\n- it is a unit vector (i.e., its magnitude equals $1$), and\n- it always points in the outward direction.\n\nSince $\\mathbf n$ must be normal to $C$ at *every* point, it must vary as we move around $C$. Therefore, it is usually written either as a function of the parameter space (i.e., $\\mathbf n(t)$) or as a function of the coordinates of the points on $C$ (i.e., $\\mathbf n(x,y)$).\n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the closed, positively oriented ellipse $C$, parameterized as\n\n$[MATH: \\mathbf r(t) = 3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).]$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1401/Source/Images/480a386111d0649afef49e31ff21b21c.png)\n\nFor convenience, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the vector $\\mathbf n$ for this curve. To do this, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$.\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) \\\\ y^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3\\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA typical tangent vector for our curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1401/Source/Images/629dab8d0a63b38ee3a39515cb45fe68.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1401/Source/Images/54a7ab966461346aaca4262674b9f479.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ in an outward-pointing normal vector, but it is not a unit vector. Therefore, we need to normalize it.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf r'(t) \\mid \\mid = \\sqrt{(3\\sin t)^2 + (2\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (n^(~)(t))/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid) \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos t \\\\ 3\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= (2\\cos ti + 3\\sin tj)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\cos ti + 3\\sin tj}{\\sqrt{9\\sin^{2} t + 4\\cos^{2} t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}}\n$$\n\ngives us an outward-pointing unit normal vector to every point on our ellipse."] [17592186107054 1029 "When curves are *negatively* oriented, the procedure of finding the outward-pointing unit normal vector is almost the same.\n\nThe only difference is the second step. If $C$ is negatively oriented (i.e., oriented *clockwise*), we must rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1401/Source/Images/9da1bd93090395f677d8a9e1c8876050.png)\n\nLet's take a look at an example."] [17592186107055 1029 "Let's consider a general, closed curve $C$ in the plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + y(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nWe will assume that $C$ is oriented counterclockwise. Our goal is to derive a general formula for $\\mathbf n$.\n\nAs always, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathbf n$, we follow our usual three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$.\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) \\\\ y^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf r'(t) \\mid \\mid = \\sqrt{(x'(t))^2 + (y'(t))^2}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (1)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)\\begin{bmatrix}y^{′}(t) \\\\ -x^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= (y^{′}(t))/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)i - (x^{′}(t))/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)j \\\\\n&= (y^{′}(t)i - x^{′}(t)j)/(\\sqrt{(x^{′}(t))^{2} + (y^{′}(t))^{2}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107056 1029 "There is specific terminology that we use when discussing samples from a population.\n\n- The individual units of the population under consideration are called **sampling units**.\n- Sampling units are ordered in a list called a **sampling frame**. The sampling frame contains all sampling units in a population and is used to create a sample.\n\nFor example, suppose we were collecting data about the ages of students in a high school. In that case, the sampling units are the individual students, and the sampling frame might be a list containing the details of every student in a school.\n\nWhen selecting a sample from a population, we must be careful not to introduce **bias** into the sample. Bias is any way the sample fails to represent the population from which it was drawn.\n\nOne way to avoid bias is through **random sampling**. In random sampling, each sampling unit is chosen randomly from the sampling frame so that every sampling unit has an equal chance of being selected.\n\nA sample is **representative** of the population if it is not biased."] [17592186107057 1029 "It is common to conduct a **sample survey** to get around issues related to conducting censuses. With a survey, we gather information from a small subset of the population.\n\nFor example, rather than measuring the height of every willow tree in England, we could measure the height of all the willow trees in one of England's parks.\n\nThere are some advantages to conducting a sample:\n\n- Provided that a sample is **representative** of the corresponding population, we can use the information gathered from the sample to draw conclusions about the population as a whole.\n- Samples are cheaper and faster to conduct than censuses.\n- Samples are helpful when conducting an investigation that results in the destruction of the quantities being sampled. For example, using a sample to test the lifespan of a particular battery brand would require us to use up only a relatively small number of batteries from the population.\n- It is typically easier to access all the data needed for a sample.\n\nWhile samples provide many benefits, there are also some disadvantages:\n\n- There is uncertainty from the fact that there is natural variation between different samples.\n- If the sample is **biased**, it will not truly represent the population. We'll discuss bias in more detail a little later.\n\nWhen conducting a survey, we need to be sure that we're only considering a subset of the population of interest."] [17592186107058 1029 "A **discrete random variable** is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nNote that an interval of real numbers cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nFor example, the result of rolling a $6$-sided dice is a discrete random variable because its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $1,2,3,4,5,6$.\n\nOn the other hand, the temperature outside is *not* a discrete random variable because its possible values form an interval of real numbers. The temperature could be anywhere between, say, $50^\\circ \\, \\textrm F$ and $90^\\circ \\, \\textrm F$, and the interval of real numbers $[50, 90]$ cannot be listed in a sequence."] [17592186107059 1029 "In general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function for a random variable $X$ on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to the conditions above.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$ states that any possible outcome for $X$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1$.\n- The condition $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ must add up to $1$."] [17592186107060 1029 "The rule of the lazy statistician states that if $Y = r(X)$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x)\n$$\n\nNote the following points:\n\n- The reason for the name \"the rule of the lazy statistician\" is that the rule is sometimes mistaken as a definition when, in fact, it is a statement that requires rigorous proof (we won't do that here).\n- The rule is sometimes called the **law of the unconscious statistician** (or **LOTUS**).\n- In general $\\textrm E[r(X)] \\ne r(\\textrm E[X])$.\n\nThe rule has some nice intuition behind it. We demonstrate with a simple example.\n\nSuppose a spinner has four sides, labeled $1$ through $4$. Let the random variable $X$ equal the result when the spinner is spun once, and let the function $Y = r(X)$ denote the profit made when the spinner lands on $X = x$. To calculate the *expected* profit on each spin, we multiply each possible payout by the probability of that payout occurring and sum over all possible values of $X$.\n\nLet's take a look at another example."] [17592186107061 1029 "A **ray** is similar to a line but has a starting point and extends infinitely in one direction only. An example of a ray is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1378/Source/Images/de454796918b858fbd8a32f4dfda3230.png)\n\nA ray that starts at the point $P$ and passes through a point $Q$ is expressed as\n$\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{PQ}$.\n\nWe use the \"$\\longrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **one** direction. Notice that the arrow's tail is above the starting point $P$.\n\nSimilar to lines, rays have no width but are infinite in length."] [17592186107062 1029 "A **segment** is part of a line bounded by two points, called the **endpoints** of the segment. An example of a segment is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1378/Source/Images/be68316ca18af4f4882f231ed898c35b.png)\n\nA segment with endpoints $A$ and $B$ is denoted $\\overline{AB}$."] [17592186107063 1029 "The sum of the interior angles of *any* triangle equals $180^\\circ$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1319/Source/Images/bfef48dc17848f1218359e4abd90b6ba.png)\n\nAs it turns out, there is a general rule for the sum of the interior angles that applies to all polygons:\n\n> *The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)$.*\n\nFor example, every triangle has ${\\color{blue}3}$ sides. Let's substitute\n\n$$\nn={\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\ninto the formula to check that it works:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (3 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 180^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ$, which we know is correct.\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186107064 1029 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)\n$$\n\nEvery quadrilateral has\n\n$$\nn={\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nsides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (4 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral equals $360^\\circ$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1319/Source/Images/1ffd9078792c35ba08fca6cd9a49708e.png)"] [17592186107065 1029 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)\n$$\n\nEvery pentagon has\n\n$$\nn={\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nsides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (5 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 540^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon equals $540^\\circ$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1319/Source/Images/d610e608d1798370efcdb09303497e93.png)\n\nWe can use the same formula to deduce the sum of the interior angles of hexagons, heptagons, octagons, and so forth."] [17592186107066 1029 "Recall that a random variable $X$ is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A random variable can take on one of many values from a set $S$, called the range or support of $X$.\n\nA **continuous random variable** is a random variable whose set of possible values $S$ is given by an interval of real numbers.\n\nFor example, the height of a randomly selected person could be modeled as a continuous random variable because its possible values form an interval of real numbers. For modeling purposes, we usually assume that the height of a randomly selected person could be *any number* in the real interval $(0,\\infty)$, although we could also restrict this to a finite interval $[a,b]$ if we wanted.\n\nOn the other hand, the result of rolling a $6$-sided die is *not* a continuous random variable because its possible values are $1,2,3,4,5,6$, which does *not* correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nPhysical quantities, such as mass, length, area, volume, time, fluid concentration, etc, are usually modeled as continuous variables.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1347/Source/Images/781f1ffe0fc00fc639898f5232d6c445.png)"] [17592186107067 1029 "The **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ such that the probability that $X$ lies in the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nThus, the probability that $X$ lies between $a$ and $b$ can be interpreted as the area bounded between $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [a,b]$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1347/Source/Images/cfbfa31ad895a8e3b450fc81595808ac.png)\n\nIn general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function over a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $6$. In this case, the pdf of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{6},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that any number between $0$ and $6$ has an equal chance of being selected, and no number outside this interval can be selected.\n\nNote that this is a valid pdf because it's always nonnegative, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{S}f(x)dx &= ∫_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6}x]_{0}^{6} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 6] - [\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 0] \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability that our randomly selected number is no greater than $3$, we integrate the pdf over the interval $[0,3]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 \\le X \\le 3) &= ∫_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6}x]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 3] - [\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result matches our intuition. Since every number has an equal chance of being selected, and $[0,3]$ is half the length of $[0,6]$, we must have\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq X \\leq 3) = \\dfrac12\n$$"] [17592186107068 1029 "The probability density function of a continuous random variable $X$ is somewhat analogous to the probability *mass* function of a discrete random variable in the sense that it describes the probability distribution of $X$.\n\nHowever, unlike the case with discrete random variables, probability density is *not* the same as probability! Instead, we can think of a pdf as a tool for computing probabilities for continuous random variables.\n\nThere are two counterintuitive yet important points to be aware of when it comes to interpreting a pdf:\n\n- *The probability that a continuous random variable takes on any particular value is always equal to $0$.* Continuing the previous example, the probability that our random variable takes on the value $X=5$ is equal to\n$P(X = 5) = P(5 \\leq X \\leq 5) = \\int_5^5 \\dfrac{1}{6} \\, \\textrm dx = 0$.\nIn general, for any value $a$ and any continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$, we have\n$P(X = a) = P(a \\leq X \\leq a) = \\int_a^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0$.\n- *The values of a probability density function may be greater than $1$.* For example, let $Y$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$. Then, the pdf of $Y$ is given by\n$f(y) = 2, \\qquad y \\in \\left[0, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right]$.\nThis pdf tells us that all numbers between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ are equally likely. Again, $f(y)$ is nonnegative, and\n$\\int_0^{1/2} f(y) \\, \\textrm d y = \\int_0^{1/2} 2 \\, \\textrm d y = 1$.\nSo, $f(y)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nLastly, note that since the probability that a continuous random variable takes on a particular value is always equal to $0$, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of a particular interval. In other words, for any continuous random variable $X$, we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X < b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$"] [17592186107069 1029 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute derivatives of composite functions when given function data at specific points.\n\nFor example, suppose we know the values of two functions, $f(x), g(x)$, and their derivatives at the points $x=1$ and $x=2$. This data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{violet}-4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $\\color{red}5$ | $2$ | $-3$ |\n\nNow, let's define a new function $h(x)$ as\n\n$$\nh(x)=f(g(x))\n$$\n\nBy the chain rule, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x)\n$$\n\nTo compute, for example, $h'(1)$, we substitute $x=1$ into the expression for $h'(x)$ above.\n\n$$\nh'(1) = f'(g(1)) \\cdot g'(1)\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate this derivative by substituting some values from the table above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{′}(1) &= f^{′}(g(1)) \\cdot g^{′}(1) \\\\\n&= f^{′}(2) \\cdot g^{′}(1) \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-4) \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh'(1) = -20\n$$"] [17592186107070 1029 "Let's now suppose we have the following data regarding the function $f(x)$ and its derivative $f'(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf(-1)={\\color{blue}2}, \\qquad\\qquad f'(-1)={\\color{red}-8}\n$$\n\nLet's use this data to evaluate the following derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left.\\left(\\dfrac{1}{f(x)}\\right)\\right|_{x=-1}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find an expression for the derivative using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}((1)/(f(x))) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(f(x))^{-1} \\\\\n&= - (f(x))^{-2} \\cdot f^{′}(x) \\\\\n&= - (1)/((f(x))^{2}) \\cdot f^{′}(x) \\\\\n&= - (f^{′}(x))/((f(x))^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, evaluating this expression at $x = -1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}((1)/(f(x))) \\mid _{x =-1} &= - (f^{′}(-1))/((f(-1))^{2}) \\\\\n&= - ((-8))/(2^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107071 1029 "The **side-angle-side (SAS) similarity criterion** can be used as a shortcut to prove that two triangles are similar. It states the following:\n\n> *Two triangles are similar if the ratio of the lengths of two pairs of corresponding sides is the same, and the angles included by these sides are congruent.*\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the two triangles shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1366/Source/Images/423d4d678d4595432188ec0e8eb6ff91.png)\n\nWe see that we have a pair of congruent angles,\n$\\angle{B}\\cong \\angle{F}$.\n\nAlso, the ratios of the side lengths that include these congruent angles are equal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AB}{EF} &= \\frac{4}{8} = \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{BC}{FG} &= \\frac{5}{10} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the SAS criterion guarantees that the two triangles are similar, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\sim \\triangle EFG\n$$"] [17592186107072 1029 "Recall that for $x >0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{x}) = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this to all values\n\n$$\nx\\neq 0\n$$\n\nby including an absolute value inside the natural logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{\\mid x \\mid}) = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral of the reciprocal function is\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln \\mid x \\mid + C\n$$"] [17592186107073 1029 "For any constant $C$, we can always write $C = \\ln K$, where $K>0$ is some other constant. Then, using the laws of logarithms, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{x}dx &= \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x & + C \\\\ = \\ln & x\\end{vmatrix}+ \\ln K \\\\\n&= \\ln (K \\mid x \\mid)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107074 1029 "In the diagram below, $\\overline{AB}$ is a chord, the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ is the tangent to the circle at $B$, and\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB = 80^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1406/Source/Images/40c8a82daba4d2167536ed66c2939acd.png)\n\nThe tangent and chord form the *acute* angle $\\angle ABQ$. The relationship between this angle and the *minor* arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is given by the **tangent-chord theorem.**\n\n> *The measure of the angle formed by a chord and a tangent passing through one of the chord's endpoints equals half the measure of the intercepted arc.*\n\nWe'll prove this theorem at the end of the lesson.\n\nIn this case, the tangent-chord theorem tell us that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{ABQ} = \\dfrac{1}{2}m\\overset{\\frown}{AB}\n$$\n\nThe measure of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ equals the measure of its central angle $\\angle{AOB}$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ABQ &= \\frac{1}{2}mAB^(⌢) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}m∠AOB \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 80^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- In the example above, the tangent-chord theorem related the *acute* angle formed by the tangent and chord and the *minor* intercepted arc.\n- We can also use the tangent-chord theorem to relate the *obtuse* angle formed by the tangent and chord and the *major* intercepted arc. We'll study this case later in the lesson.\n\nLet's get some more practice at applying the tangent-chord theorem."] [17592186107075 1029 "Let's now prove the tangent-chord theorem. Consider the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1406/Source/Images/9fa43c975d818b6d26a4a3f18d651a67.png)\n\nOur goal is to show that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{PAB} = \\dfrac12 m\\overset{\\frown}{AB}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{OA}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are radii, they are congruent. Hence, the triangle $\\triangle AOB$ is an isosceles triangle. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAO = m∠ABO &= \\frac{1}{2}(180^{∘} - m∠AOB) \\\\\n&= 90^{∘} - \\frac{1}{2}m∠AOB\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the tangent at $A$ is perpendicular to the radius $\\overline{OA}$, the angles $\\angle{PAB}$ and $\\angle{BAO}$ are complementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PAB &= 90^{∘} - m∠BAO \\\\\n&= 90^{∘} - (90^{∘} - \\frac{1}{2}m∠AOB) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}m∠AOB\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the measure of the central angle $\\angle AOB$ equals the measure of the intercepted arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$, we conclude that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{PAB} = \\dfrac12 m\\overset{\\frown}{AB}\n$$"] [17592186107076 1029 "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1281/Source/Images/29139937c3a52324919577e8973a538e.png)\n\nWe can approximate the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1281/Source/Images/13b2995c70bd99847493e9ace4e179ce.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx [f(2)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]1[/math]st rectangle) + [f(3)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]2[/math]nd rectangle) + [f(4)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]3[/math]rd rectangle); = [2^{2} \\cdot 1] + [3^{2} \\cdot 1] + [4^{2} \\cdot 1]; = 4 + 9 + 16; = 29\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **right Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*right* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4]$, the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area (see the picture above)."] [17592186107077 1029 "In general, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[a,b]$ can be approximated using a right Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1281/Source/Images/b557451067ef766d284853bb5854864a.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx f(x_1)\\Delta x+f(x_2)\\Delta x+f(x_3)\\Delta x+ \\cdots + f(x_n) \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of rectangles and the step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the $\\Delta x$, which gives the following general formula:\n\n$$\nheights\n$$"] [17592186107078 1029 "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta{x}$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nThe first thing we note is that the domain of the function $y=f(x)$ is *partitioned* as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} [1,4] &= [1, 1.5] \\cup [1.5, 2.5] \\cup [2.5, 4]. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1281/Source/Images/22b27a2d788b7111178adea9862f3120.png)\n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our right Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the right endpoint of each interval.\n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\mathcal{A} &\\approx {\\color{red}2} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}1.5-1}) + {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2.5-1.5}) + {\\color{red}8} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-2.5}) \\\\ &= {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5} +{\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1}+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5} \\\\ &=1+6+12 \\\\ &= 19. \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107079 1029 "Recall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\| \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nNow, if a surface is given by the explicit equation $z=g(x,y)$ over a plane region $D$ in Cartesian coordinates, then the canonical parametrization of this surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \\langle x,\\: y, \\: g(x,y) \\rangle, \\qquad (x,y) \\in D\n$$\n\nMoreover, we know that under the canonical parametrization, the magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′}‖ &= \\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$"] [17592186107080 1029 "Consider the triangle below with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,1)$ and $(2,3)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/99fccef4611b8fcb6b3961cf37d73e71.png)\n\nWhat is the image of this triangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represented by the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all vertices of our triangle written as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,0)$. Notice that these points lie on the same line. Two of them even coincide!\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the following line segment:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/942c0ed880bd56c0318d7fbf0588981e.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ and region $D$, we have\n$\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = \\mid \\det(T) \\mid \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)$.\nSince $\\mid \\det(T) \\mid = 0$ in this case, any object applied to $\\mathbf T$ collapses to an object with zero area.\n- Also, the fact that $\\mid \\det(T) \\mid = 0$ tells us that $\\mathbf T$ has no inverse transformation. Therefore, it is impossible to recover the original triangle knowing only its image and the matrix $T$."] [17592186107081 1029 "Given a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with an associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$, there are three possibilities regarding the type of object formed under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ by a two-dimensional preimage.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the three-sided polygon $\\mathcal P$ with vertices $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$ and $(2,1)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/ae508c15cb767d68db89d7463f09a17f.png)\n\nLet's describe the three possible types of object formed by the image of $\\mathcal P{:}$\n\n- Case where $T$ is non-singular:Let $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nWe can easily check that $T$ is non-singular by computing its determinant:\n$det(T)|=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = 2 \\cdot 0 - 1 \\cdot 1; =-1$\nTo find the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n$TX|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/b95ce660f76f3cb28bf70c9af98ae9fe.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:Let $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n$det(T)|=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}; = 2 \\cdot 3 - 6 \\cdot 1; = 0$\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get\n$TX|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/2de537c0c5f9e5bf868067dcd8dcbdb7.png)\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:Let $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get\n$TX|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/9b0cfda3c64dd9acf06c0c9b5ceb6c0e.png)"] [17592186107082 1029 "In general, for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0$, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0$, we have two additional subcases:If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0)$, the origin of the coordinate system."] [17592186107083 1029 "Suppose we are given a point $P$ with position vector $\\mathbf{p}$, and another vector $\\mathbf{v}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1376/Source/Images/864b778401c952feb7ce11b7c884d1bc.png)\n\nThe **vector equation** of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nr = p + tv, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is often called the **direction vector** of the line, while the vector $\\mathbf{r}$ denotes the position vector of an arbitrary point on the line.\n\nFor example, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe vector equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107084 1029 "There is a geometric intuition behind the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1376/Source/Images/9d52762911c1c854095be3160f00bb4e.png)\n\nTo get to any point $A(x,y,z)$ on the desired line, starting from the origin $O$, we can\n\n- first travel along the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ to get to the point $P$ on the line, and\n- then travel along the vector $t \\mathbf{v}$ to get to $A$. (The scalar $t$ represents how far we travel forward or backward along the line in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}$.)"] [17592186107085 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1376/Source/Images/ebc3359b51513d2cf52986e9f1f66682.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find a vector equation of the line *segment* whose endpoints are $P(3,-1,7)$ and $Q(3,4,4)$.\n\nTo do this, we need to find the vector equation of the corresponding line and then restrict the domain of the parameter $t$.\n\nFirst, we need to find a direction vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= PQ^(→) \\\\\n&= OQ^(→) - OP^(→) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThis equation describes every point on the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$.\n\nTo restrict this to points on the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ only, we set $t\\in [0,1]$. Thus, the vector equation of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t - 1 \\\\ 7 - 3t\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWriting the equation of a line segment in this way is sometimes called **parametrizing** the line segment since each point on the segment corresponds to a single parameter $t$.\n\n- Notice that $t=0$ gives us the position vector of $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(0) - 1 \\\\ 7 - 3(0)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, $t=1$ gives us the position vector of $Q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(1) - 1 \\\\ 7 - 3(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince increasing $t$ moves our vector $\\mathbf r (t)$ from $P$ to $Q$, we say that the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is **traversed** *from* the point $P$ *to* the point $Q$ under this parametrization."] [17592186107086 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1322/Source/Images/05c0a4c42e6e2e3ae0142416ada72e8a.png)\n\nConsider the regular pentagon above. What is the measure of each interior angle?\n\nRecall that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)$.\n\nAll interior angles in a regular polygon are congruent. So, to calculate the measure of each interior angle, we find the sum of the interior angles and divide by the number of angles.\n\nA pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of its interior angles is\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\times (5 - 2) = 540^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of each interior angle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{540^\\circ}{5} = 108^\\circ\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1322/Source/Images/4fee5ef181378c4588a5e6e5d9b5827b.png)"] [17592186107087 1029 "A **regular polygon** is a polygon where all sides are congruent (have the same length), and all interior angles are congruent (have the same measure).\n\nSome examples of regular polygons are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1322/Source/Images/a875c8d6b065bffdf1c52065e9fe5163.png)\n\nSome examples of polygons that are **NOT** regular are as follows:\n\n- The rectangle below is **NOT** regular since the sides are not all congruent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1322/Source/Images/742638e67ecf82274c0cffeeb2dd29a5.png)\n\n- The rhombus below is **NOT** regular since the interior angles are not all congruent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1322/Source/Images/8d63a4b5ea47a50367ad8e2e37252a11.png)\n\n- A $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle is **NOT** regular since neither the sides nor the angles are all congruent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1322/Source/Images/ce714ddd178c0fa90d5a47226c5d3e17.png)"] [17592186107088 1029 "Consider the polygon $ABCD$, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is extended beyond the vertex $B$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1321/Source/Images/80dafbae6db90d5e7c1a4b916c80ace1.png)\n\nWe call $\\color{blue}\\angle{CBE}$ the **exterior angle** of the vertex $B$.\n\nGiven a polygon's vertex, the interior and exterior angles are supplementary. So, for the vertex $B$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{ABC}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{CBE}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe have the following theorem:\n\n> The measures of the exterior angles of any simple polygon sum to $360^\\circ$.\n\nWe'll show why this theorem is true at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's label the exterior angles of our polygon below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1321/Source/Images/7e40510cdaf70150310e402ee233faa3.png)\n\nAccording to our theorem, these four angles sum to $360^\\circ$.\n\n$$\na+b+c+d = 360^\\circ\n$$"] [17592186107089 1029 "Why do the exterior angles of a polygon always sum to $360^\\circ$?\n\nSuppose we have an $n$-sided polygon. Then, we have the following:\n\n- There are $n$ pairs of interior-exterior angles, each pair being supplementary (i.e., their measures add to $180^\\circ)$.\n- Therefore, the sum of the measures of *all* interior-exterior pairs equals ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}$.\n- In a previous lesson, we saw that the sum of the *interior* angles equals ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}$.\n\nTherefore, to get the sum of the *exterior* angles, we subtract ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}$ from ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSum of Exterior Angles &= n \\cdot 180^{∘} - (n - 2) \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= n \\cdot 180^{∘} - n \\cdot 180^{∘} + 2 \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= n \\cdot 180^{∘} - n \\cdot 180^{∘} + 2 \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis proves that the sum of the exterior angles of a (simple) polygon equals $360^\\circ$."] [17592186107090 1029 "To find the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms, we must usually apply the following:\n\n- The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by\n$a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d$\nwhere $a_n$ is the $n$th term, $a_1$ is the first term, and $d$ is the common difference.\n- We can find the common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms using\n$d = \\frac{a_m-a_k}{m-k}$\nwhere $a_m$ is the $m$th term, and $a_k$ is the $k$th term.\n\nLet's see some examples."] [17592186107091 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1322/Source/Images/731acfd278b510fa10790ea7715bf444.png)\n\nConsider the regular hexagon above. What is the measure of each *exterior* angle?\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The exterior angles in a regular polygon are all congruent.\n- The sum of the exterior angles of *any* convex polygon equals $360^\\circ$.\n\nSo, to find the measure of the exterior angles of a regular polygon, we divide $360^\\circ$ by the number of sides.\n\nSince a regular hexagon has $n=6$ sides, the measure of each exterior angle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{6} = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1322/Source/Images/0092181d92d8ad9d379b9dd3bad5f218.png)"] [17592186107092 1029 "The **vector projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the portion that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends along $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{b} &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1295/Source/Images/70c45a3fa72847dea43944c218fe8fcd.png)\n\nThe starting point of the vector projection is $O$, and the ending point will be the point $P$ that lies on the line containing $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1295/Source/Images/c1f6a3e248a3fcd47b9077790ee3ebb8.png)\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ is called the **vector projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$. It is denoted as $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}$, and it is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= OP \\cdot u \\\\\n&= comp_{b}a \\cdot u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} = \\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nis the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nFirst, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\mid a \\mid \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{b}\\, \\mid} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= comp_{b}a \\cdot u \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107093 1029 "We have been using the following formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} =\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can write an alternative formula using the formula for the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the original formula for the vector projection, and also substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{\\mid \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\begin{vmatrix}b &} \\cdot \\frac{b}{\\mid b\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid b \\mid ^{2}}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107094 1029 "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then by projecting $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$), we obtain the zero vector.\n\nFor example, the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto a perpendicular vector $\\mathbf{b}$ looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1295/Source/Images/f3bb4c9d8854af35f141a1b60005492d.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals the zero vector if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*"] [17592186107095 1029 "Whenever we have a fraction that contains a square root in the denominator, such as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nwe often want to find an equivalent fraction that has no square root in the denominator.\n\nTo find this equivalent fraction, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the square root in the denominator. This process is known as **rationalizing the denominator**.\n\nFor example, for the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nwe can remove the square root from the denominator by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} &=  \\\\\n(3\\sqrt{2})/((\\sqrt{2})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when we rationalize the denominator in the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nwe get the equivalent fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nthat has no square root in the denominator."] [17592186107096 1029 "We've made use of the following theorem throughout this lesson.\n\n> *The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)$.*\n\nThis formula works because any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles using diagonals that meet at one fixed vertex of the polygon.\n\nLet's illustrate this for a few different polygons.\n\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1319/Source/Images/fc836eb3d4f59a4a36b244ebbcedc895.png) | **Quadrilateral**<br><br><br>Number of vertices: $4$<br><br><br>Number of triangles: $4 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$<br><br><br>Sum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} = 360^\\circ$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1319/Source/Images/34f70d763b81d4d801b6684b1528c002.png) | **Pentagon**<br><br><br>Number of vertices: $5$<br><br><br>Number of triangles: $5 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}$<br><br><br>Sum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} = 540^\\circ$ |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1319/Source/Images/517bad687ea7c1d82eb717da81d0f98d.png) | **Hexagon**<br><br><br>Number of vertices: $6$<br><br><br>Number of triangles: $6 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{4}}$<br><br><br>Sum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{4}} = 720^\\circ$ |\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of each polygon is the same as the sum of the interior angles of the corresponding triangles.\n\nEach triangle has interior angles that sum to $180^\\circ$, and any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles in the manner shown above. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles in an $n$-sided polygon is\n\n$$\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2)\n$$"] [17592186107097 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1353/Source/Images/8ab42f16e1df1d7aa270ea35b39431f5.png)\n\nWe'll now derive the formula for the area of a trapezoid. Consider a trapezoid with bases of length $a, b$, and height $h$, as shown above.\n\nLet's now make another copy of the figure, rotate it $180^\\circ$, and place it next to the original.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1353/Source/Images/09931ec16331a32761c383b244d4fa71.png)\n\nWe now have a parallelogram with a base of length $a+b$ and a height of $h$. The area of this parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area of Parallelogram} = \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height} = (a+b) \\times h\n$$\n\nTo find the area of the original trapezoid, we divide the above value in half:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area of Trapezoid} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Area of Parallelogram}}{2} = \\dfrac{(a+b) \\cdot h}{2}\n$$"] [17592186107098 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1399/Source/Images/5cfc17a1b7b188656d7f97ecb4e7a1e6.png)\n\nWe'll now derive the formula for the area of a parallelogram. Consider a parallelogram with base $b$ and height $h$, as shown above.\n\nLet's now cut the small triangle along the height on the right-hand side and place it on the other side of the original figure.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1399/Source/Images/9a09bf078fe5c24d8d01ef2d94eb3406.png)\n\nWe now have a rectangle with a base of length $b$ and a height of $h$. The area of this rectangle equals the area of our parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area of Parallelogram} = \\textrm{Area of Rectangle} = \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height} = b \\times h\n$$"] [17592186107099 1029 "Consider an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$, then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results.\n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent."] [17592186107100 1029 "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand that the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1285/Source/Images/64af6629c431159c6711f4513a638ed2.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$, consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1285/Source/Images/afba77446d85db03d103433487bec49b.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}$, and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\, \\mathbf{a} & = O P \\\\  & = | \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, | \\cos   \\theta \\\\  & = 2 \\cos   ( \\frac{\\pi }{4} ) \\\\  & = 2 \\cdot  \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & = \\sqrt{2}\\end{array}$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= \\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid \\cos\\theta\n$$"] [17592186107101 1029 "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= \\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{a} \\cdot  \\mathbf{b} & = | \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, | \\, | \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, | \\cos   \\theta \\\\ \\cos   \\theta & = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot  \\mathbf{b}}{| \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, | \\, | \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, |}\\end{array}$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\, \\mathbf{a} & = | \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, | \\cos   \\theta \\\\  & = | \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, | \\cdot  \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot  \\mathbf{b}}{| \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, | \\, | \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, |} \\\\  & = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot  \\mathbf{b}}{| \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, |} .\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\, \\mathbf{a} & = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot  \\mathbf{b}}{| \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, |} .\\end{array}$$"] [17592186107102 1029 "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$) is equal to $0$.\n\nFor example, consider the following illustration. Notice that, since $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ doesn't extend in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$ at all. So, the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1285/Source/Images/343c7ce96cd3598d0a81cd8f5e38ae9f.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals $0$ if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*"] [17592186107103 1029 "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1285/Source/Images/a246cb0b361184d4b0f402de21f2919c.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, where it is known that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse.\n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\, \\mathbf{a} & = | \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, | \\cos   \\theta \\\\  & = 2 \\cos   ( {135}^{\\circ } ) \\\\  & = 2 \\cdot  ( - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} ) \\\\  & = - \\sqrt{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nIn general, if\n\n$$\n90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nthen $\\cos\\theta <0$ and the scalar projection will be negative."] [17592186107104 1029 "The shape below consists of $6$ line segments defined by the $6$ points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F$. These line segments form a loop.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1317/Source/Images/3c3a0da8555d498ba11b8cabf3823fb5.png)\n\nA chain of straight line segments that form a loop is called a **polygon**.\n\nThe line segments are the **sides** of the polygon, and the endpoints of the sides are the **vertices**.\n\nFor this polygon:\n\n- The $6$ vertices are $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F$.\n- The $6$ sides are $\\overline{AB}$, $\\overline{BC}$, $\\overline{CD}$, $\\overline{DE}$, $\\overline{EF}$, and $\\overline{AF}$.\n\nTo denote a particular polygon, we start at one vertex and loop around the polygon, reading off the vertices in order. So, the polygon above can be denoted $ABCDEF$.\n\nThe singular form of the word *vertices* is **vertex**. So, $A$ is a vertex, $B$ is a vertex, etc."] [17592186107105 1029 "The name of a polygon is determined by the number of sides it has.\n\nThe polygon below has $6$ sides. A six-sided polygon is called a **hexagon**.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1317/Source/Images/01871ba587faffb564854a5af6ac4edf.png)\n\nThe names of the polygons with up to ten sides are given below:\n\n| Number of sides | Name |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $3$ | Triangle |\n| $4$ | Quadrilateral |\n| $5$ | Pentagon |\n| $6$ | Hexagon |\n| $7$ | Heptagon |\n| $8$ | Octagon |\n| $9$ | Nonagon |\n| $10$ | Decagon |"] [17592186107106 1029 "Consider the pentagon $ABCDE$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1317/Source/Images/89e78efde6945aa5b64e722ae7e49f06.png)\n\nWe have the following definitions:\n\n- Two *sides* are **adjacent** (or **consecutive**) if they have a common vertex.For example, $\\color[rgb]{0.753,0.494,0.643}\\overline{BC}$ is adjacent to $\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{AB}$ *and* ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{CD}}$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1317/Source/Images/17693d96d14395030c384b8f6fa137fe.png)\n- Two *vertices* are **adjacent** if there is a side joining them. For example, the vertex $E$ is adjacent to the vertices $D$ and $A$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1317/Source/Images/4c8ccaf84e8329a8416da22e51bfdf14.png)\n- A **diagonal** is a line segment that joins two non-adjacent vertices. For example, ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{AC}}}, {\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BD}}}$, and ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BE}}}$ are diagonals of our pentagon.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1317/Source/Images/21f7365745befd38dec5f6aa3ffdcfcf.png)"] [17592186107107 1029 "When finding the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$, we sometimes find that the resulting projection vector points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1295/Source/Images/1a9f17a8c9a9ad3438b1098867c60a0f.png)\n\nConsider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, where it is known that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2,\\qquad \\mid \\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|=3,\\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nFirst, notice that the angle between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ is obtuse. This means we expect the projection vector to point in the opposite direction to that of $\\mathbf b$.\n\nNext, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\mid a \\mid \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (135^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the scalar projection is negative.\n\nThen, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{b}\\, \\mid} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= comp_{b}a \\cdot u \\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis vector points in the direction opposite to $\\mathbf{b}$, as expected."] [17592186107108 1029 "We can categorize polygons in different ways.\n\nA polygon is **simple** if its boundary does not intersect itself. Otherwise, the polygon is **complex**.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1317/Source/Images/557b0c5adc92ec5855816264827a3006.png)\n\nA polygon is **convex** if it has no angles pointing inwards. Otherwise, if the polygon has angles pointing inwards, we call it **concave**.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1317/Source/Images/b0dbbad160a0b4dbf4c7a9345831ce90.png)\n\nIt might help to remember that a con**cave** polygon looks a bit like the entrance to a **cave** when viewed from the outside.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1317/Source/Images/5193ae62a3313243b9d8a2b3856948ff.png)\n\n**Note**: A more technical way to define a convex polygon is to say that all of its *internal angles* are less than $180^\\circ$. If you're not sure what an internal angle is yet, don't worry! We'll learn more about the internal angles of a polygon in future lessons."] [17592186111734 1029 "A **median** in a triangle is a line segment joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $M$ is the midpoint of the side $\\overline{AB}$. Therefore, $\\overline{CM}$ is a median of $\\triangle ABC$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1524/Source/Images/2b4fa1ffd45c5b6834e136ee61c9e3d0.png)\n\nAny triangle has exactly three medians, one for each side. All three medians of $\\triangle ABC$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1524/Source/Images/159b5c2152ad50ae9fec652851a72c36.png)\n\nYou'll notice that the medians intersect at a single point. This is true for *all* triangles. We'll discuss this in more detail shortly."] [17592186111735 1029 "We're now ready to state and prove the main result of this lesson.\n\n*Theorem:*\n\n> *All circles are similar.*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n> *Consider a circle $\\mathcal{C}_1$ of radius $R_1$ centered at $A(x_1,y_1)$ and a circle $\\mathcal{C}_2$ of radius $R_2$ centered at $B(x_2,y_2)$. Let $\\mathcal{U}$ be the unit circle centered at the origin.*\n> *First, we will show that*\n> $\\mathcal{C}_1 \\sim \\mathcal{U}$.\n> *For the circle $\\mathcal{C}_1$ of radius $R_1$ centered at $A(x_1,y_1)$, applying the translation $(x,y)\\mapsto (x - x_1, y-y_1)$ followed by a dilation of scale factor $\\dfrac{1}{R_1}$ and center $O$ maps $\\mathcal C_1$ onto $\\mathcal U$.*\n> *Next, we'll show that*\n> $\\mathcal{C}_2 \\sim \\mathcal{U}$.\n> *For the circle $\\mathcal{C}_2$ of radius $R_2$ centered at $B(x_2,y_2)$, applying the translation $(x,y)\\mapsto (x - x_2, y-y_2)$ followed by a dilation of scale factor $\\dfrac{1}{R_2}$ and center $O$ maps $\\mathcal C_2$ onto $\\mathcal U$.*\n> *Now, by the symmetric property of similarity,*\n> $\\mathcal{U} \\sim \\mathcal{C}_2$.\n> *Therefore, by the transitive property of similarity,*\n> $\\mathcal{C}_1 \\sim \\mathcal{C}_2$."] [17592186111736 1029 "The centroid has an important property that is often called the **centroid ratio theorem**:\n\n> *The centroid of a triangle splits any median in the proportion $2:1$, starting from the corresponding vertex.*\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $G$ is the centroid, and $\\overline{CK}$ is one of the medians.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1524/Source/Images/741048efe529fe60dd996f7aa3747528.png)\n\nAccording to the centroid theorem,\n\n$$\nCG: GK = 2: 1\n$$\n\nThe same proportion holds for the other medians of the triangle. Just remember that the longer partition always contains the vertex.\n\nWe'll prove the centroid ratio theorem in a future lesson."]}
